                "img": "https://arxiv.org/html/2610.07609/2610.07609v3/figs/teaser_v10.jpg",
                "caption": "Figure 1:PhysLDM overview.Top, Reconstruction:Ground truth (rose) versus ST-VAE reconstruction (blue) for an unseen high-resolution soft mug (11,621 nodes,>2×{>}2\\timesthe training cap). The trajectory is compressed by∼182×{\\sim}182\\timeswith only a 5.3 mm mean error. Far right: a cross-section of the final frame visualizing the internal tetrahedral structure.Bottom Left, Prediction:One-shot prediction of 64-frame trajectories for diverse out-of-distribution (OOD) objects (GSO/Toys4K) across varying materials and initial velocities (inset: initial state; main view: predicted settled state).Bottom Right, Differentiable Applications:A classic material estimation task: recovering Young’s modulusEE(blue sequence) by matching a target state (textured) via gradient descent. Furthermore, designing the throw that best reveals the material is a second-order inverse task (Sec.4.2). PhysLDM easily solves it with native support for higher-order derivatives. By contrast, all other differentiable simulators we tested either fail or are computationally prohibitive on this task.",
                "position": 338
            }
        ]
