        "header": "Appendix CStaircase Analysis Details",
        "images": [
            {
                "img": "https://arxiv.org/html/2610.07609/2610.07609v3/fig_staircase_scenes.png",
                "caption": "Figure 5:The staircase artifact on five GSO scenes. Top: ground truth at the impact frame. Bottom: deformation-change curve (defined in AppendixB.3) for ground truth and the six ST-VAEs of Table7over the impact window. DCC in Table1is the correlation of this curve with the ground-truth curve. On every scene the standard baseline, its two variants, and the adaptive-compression baseline stay below ground truth for several frames and then overshoot it in one frame, while the holistic ST-VAE follows the ground-truth curve. The direct world-coordinate baseline also follows the ground-truth curve, because it uses the same holistic temporal compression.",
                "position": 1967
            },
            {
                "img": "https://arxiv.org/html/2610.07609/2610.07609v3/fig_staircase_strip.png",
                "caption": "Figure 6:Consecutive-frame silhouette difference on scene 4 of Fig.5(a cat) for five frame pairs around the impact (red: previous frame only; blue: next frame only; grey: overlap; number: XOR over union). Ground truth and the holistic ST-VAE change steadily from frame to frame. The standard baseline and its two variants change much less than the ground truth for the first three pairs and then much more in the last two: the reconstruction holds and then snaps. The adaptive-compression baseline changes too little over all five pairs and releases the motion even later. The direct world-coordinate baseline, which uses the holistic temporal compression, follows the ground truth until the last pair.",
                "position": 1970
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "header": "Appendix DChaos in Traditional GPU Simulators",
        "images": [
            {
                "img": "https://arxiv.org/html/2610.07609/2610.07609v3/fig_chaos_grid.png",
                "caption": "Figure 7:Chaos in a traditional GPU simulator. Five reruns (columns) of thesamesoft-racket scene frombyte-identicalinputs (same mesh, initial state, material, and solver configuration), shown at four sampled frames (rows): before the drop, just after impact, mid-bounce, and the last frame of the trajectory. The runs are indistinguishable until impact and then settle into visibly different poses. The complex dynamics are chaotic, so the post-impact outcome is not a deterministic function of the initial state.",
                "position": 2091
            },
            {
                "img": "https://arxiv.org/html/2610.07609/2610.07609v3/fig_keys_grid.png",
                "caption": "Figure 8:The key scene of AppendixD.6(a held-out scene) at four sampled frames (rows; ground contact at frame3131). Columns: the two runs of the GPU solver from byte-identical inputs, which fall over in different directions; the regression prediction, which settles in between and resembles neither run; and the two diffusion samples closest to run11and to run22among the eight samples.",
                "position": 2195
            }
        ]
